<%# Confirming the address and choosing the password, reached from the link in the email. #575.

    This is where an account actually starts existing. The sign-up form takes an address and
    writes a row with no credential at all -- `password_hash` is nullable as of migrate/051,
    and the note there is where that is argued -- so until this form is posted there is
    nothing anybody can sign in to. That is the whole point of the feature rather than an
    implementation detail: the four hundred and nine rows that prompted #575 were usable
    accounts the moment a bot pressed the button, and under this flow they would have been
    rows waiting on mail nobody read.

    **The password box carries the cost of that.** On the sign-up form it said
    `autocomplete="new-password"`, which is the instruction that makes a manager offer to
    generate a credential and keep it; here the same token is on a page reached from an
    email, which is frequently a different browser from the one that filled the form in, or a
    phone. Whatever manager is present still gets asked -- that is why the token is here and
    not omitted -- but the odds that one is present are worse than they were, and app.rb's
    note on this flow is where that trade is taken deliberately rather than stumbled into.

    One box, matching views/reset-password.erb and for the reason recorded there: app.rb turns
    Rodauth's confirmation fields off, so a second box would be collected, ignored and never
    compared. Rodauth renders `password-confirm-field` here only when
    `require_password_confirmation?` is true, and it is not.

    Why a form at all, rather than confirming on the GET? Two reasons, and the first would
    hold even without a password to collect. A link in an inbox is fetched by things that are
    not the person it was sent to: spam filters, link scanners, corporate mail gateways and
    the phone's own preview all GET every URL in a message, some within seconds of delivery.
    A confirmation that happened on GET would routinely be done by a scanner, which is most of
    the evidence this feature exists to collect. The second is that a password cannot be typed
    into a GET.

    There is no hidden key field, and that is the point rather than an omission -- exactly as
    on views/reset-password.erb. Rodauth answers the emailed link with a redirect to this
    route without the query string, having put the token in the session on the way past, so
    the secret leaves the address bar and cannot escape through a Referer header on whatever
    loads next. By the time this renders, the key is session state and the form has nothing
    to carry. %>
<div class="flex min-h-full flex-col justify-center px-6 py-12 lg:px-8">
  <div class="sm:mx-auto sm:w-full sm:max-w-sm">
    <h1 class="text-center text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Choose your password</h1>
    <%== render('_form_error') %>
    <p class="mt-4 text-center text-sm text-gray-600">
      Your address is confirmed. Pick a password and the account is yours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 sm:mx-auto sm:w-full sm:max-w-sm">
    <form method="post" class="space-y-6" role="form" id="verify-account-form" autocomplete="on">
      <%== rodauth.csrf_tag %>
      <div>
        <label for="password" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Password</label>
        <div class="mt-2">
          <input id="password" name="password" type="password" autocomplete="new-password" required
                 class="<%= field_style %> block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> flex w-full justify-center bg-lime-500 px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-semibold leading-6 text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save it and sign in</button>
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
</div>
